{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014HcuHT5iUaUpQae9mpbod2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context and determine what to build next.\n\n## Step 1: Analyze Project Context\n\nThis is a Go project that re-implements Unix utilities from C source specifications, verified by differential testing against GNU reference binaries. The pipeline is: extract specs from C source → write PRDs → generate Go code → verify via differential testing.\n\n## Step 2: Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications. Re-implementations diverge in behavior. This project derives specs from C source and generates verified Go implementations.\n\n**Architecture**:\n- `cmd/` - Per-utility Go implementations\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n\n\n- `pkg/sys/` - Syscall abstractions (Darwin/Linux)\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting (columns, colors, human-readable sizes)\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n- `docs/` - Specs, PRDs, use cases, test suites\n\n**Current Implementation State**: The testing infrastructure and system abstractions are already in place, with differential testing harness, terminal handling, file info utilities, signal management, and platform-specific stat implementations for both Darwin and Linux.\n\nLooking at the actual source code, I see that ts, wc, cat, and sponge are all implemented with their test suites, but the format package still needs to be built out.\n\n**Roadmap**: ts is currently in progress with its use case being developed, while wc, cat, and sponge are queued up for the next release cycle. The ls command and du are planned for version 2.0, with extended ls features coming after that. Looking at the actual source code, I can see that ts, wc, cat, and sponge all have implementations already, which means the ARCHITECTURE document is outdated. The pieces that are genuinely missing are pkg/format (needed for the rel02.0 commands), along with cmd/ls and cmd/du themselves. So rel01.0 appears nearly complete with working code and tests, while rel01.1 has implementations but the documentation status hasn't caught up.\n\nFor rel02.0, I need to build pkg/format first since both ls and du depend on it for column alignment, color output, and human-readable formatting. Let me verify whether rel01.0's use case is actually finished or if there's still work needed there. and tests exist. Maybe the status hasn't been updated.\n\nGiven the constraint of 1 task maximum, I need to pick the most impactful next task.\n\nSince rel01.0 and rel01.1 appear to have their implementations done (source code exists), the next meaningful work is rel02.0, which starts with `pkg/format/`.\n\nLooking at prd003-format:\n- R1: Column alignment (Columns function, PadRight, PadLeft)\n- R2: ANSI color (FileTypeColor, Reset, ColorEnabled)\n- R3: Human-readable size conversion (HumanSize)\n\nThis is the foundation for ls and du. Let me create a task for implementing pkg/format.\n\nI'm breaking down the three core modules needed: columns.go for alignment logic, color.go for ANSI output handling, and humansize.go for size formatting. Estimating the line counts, these three files should total around 190-250 lines, which fits within the task constraints while keeping implementation separate from tests. range (on the lower end). Could fit in one task.\n\nBut I'm only allowed 1 task. Let me make it the implementation of pkg/format.\n\nActually, let me re-examine. The constraint says \"Do NOT exceed 1 tasks.\" So I need exactly 1 task.\n\nGiven that the implementations for rel01.0 and rel01.1 already exist, the next priority is pkg/format for rel02.0.\n\nLet me create a task for implementing pkg/format (R1, R2, R3) - the implementation only, not tests (per P2: split implementation and tests).\n\nWait, but the constraint says 250-350 lines. Let me look at what all needs to be in pkg/format: column distribution with configurable width, padding functions with multi-byte Unicode support, file type coloring with LS_COLORS defaults, terminal detection, and human-readable size formatting with both binary and SI modes. That's 12 requirements across three major feature groups.\n\nI'm estimating around 260-280 lines total, which fits the range. I'll create one task for the full pkg/format implementation. I'm anchoring the task to specific PRD requirement IDs and laying out the key design decisions: delegating TTY detection to the sys package, using GNU ls color defaults, computing column widths per-column rather than globally, limiting HumanSize to one decimal place, and using rune count for display width calculations. I'm reviewing the acceptance criteria—seven items covering the Columns function, padding operations, file type coloring, TTY detection, human-readable sizes, and code quality checks. Now I need to map out the requirements: implementing Columns per the spec, PadRight and PadLeft functions, and computing display widths using rune count rather than byte count to keep things simple. I need to consolidate these requirements into a cleaner set of implementation tasks: a Columns function that distributes entries across the maximum number of columns that fit the terminal width, padding functions for alignment, proper rune-based width calculations, color support using ANSI escape sequences with GNU ls defaults, and a ColorEnabled check that delegates to the system's TTY detection. I'm setting the GNU ls color codes for each file type—directories get blue, symlinks cyan, executables green, and special files get bold yellow or magenta depending on the type. I'll organize the implementation across three files: columns.go handles column alignment, color.go manages ANSI color codes and the ColorEnabled check via TTY detection, and humansize.go converts byte sizes to human-readable format with binary or SI units.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":128094,"cache_read_input_tokens":18565,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":128094},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"daebe5a8-f5ff-41e3-974a-afe47dde16e5","uuid":"8d42513f-388a-4877-a38d-763bfd779591"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014HcuHT5iUaUpQae9mpbod2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications. This project derives specs from C source, generates Go implementations via an AI orchestrator, and verifies parity against GNU reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). `cmd/` holds per-utility implementations, `pkg/testutils/` provides the differential testing harness, `pkg/sys/` wraps platform-divergent syscalls, `pkg/format/` provides shared output formatting, and `magefiles/` exposes build targets.\n\n**Current state**: `pkg/testutils/`, `pkg/sys/`, `cmd/ts/`, `cmd/wc/`, `cmd/cat/`, and `cmd/sponge/` all have implementations and differential tests. `pkg/format/` has a complete PRD (prd003-format) but no implementation code yet. `cmd/ls/` and `cmd/du/` have PRDs but no implementation.\n\n**Current release**: rel01.0 (ts) and rel01.1 (wc, cat, sponge) have code in place. The earliest release needing new implementation is **rel02.0** (ls core and du), which depends on `pkg/format/` — the only shared library PRD without an implementation.\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\n`pkg/format/` is the critical-path dependency for rel02.0. Both `cmd/ls/` (prd008-ls R1.1, R4.3, R5.1) and `cmd/du/` (prd009-du R2.1) depend on it. Per P5 (libraries before consumers) and P8 (libraries before consumers in ordering), this must be built before either utility. The PRD is fully specified with concrete API signatures, color codes, and formatting rules — no ambiguity remains.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Implement pkg/format column alignment, ANSI color, and human-readable size (prd003-format R1, R2, R3)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd003-format.yaml (full PRD for pkg/format -- column alignment, ANSI color, human-readable size)\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd002-sys.yaml (pkg/sys API -- IsTerminal used by ColorEnabled)\n      - pkg/sys/terminal.go (IsTerminal function signature and implementation)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (pkg/format component description and technology choices)\n      - docs/specs/dependency-map.yaml (confirms ls and du are the consumers of pkg/format)\n\n    files:\n      - path: pkg/format/columns.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"Columns, PadRight, PadLeft, displayWidth -- column alignment primitives for ls multi-column layout\"\n      - path: pkg/format/color.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"FileTypeColor, Reset, ColorEnabled -- ANSI color output with TTY-aware suppression\"\n      - path: pkg/format/humansize.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"HumanSize, HumanSizeOpts -- byte-to-human-readable conversion for ls -h and du -h\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement Columns(entries []string, termWidth int) [][]string that distributes entries into the maximum number of columns fitting within termWidth. Column widths are computed from the longest entry in each column, not the global maximum. Entries are distributed vertically (down columns, then across). Per prd003-format R1.1, R1.4.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement PadRight(s string, width int) string and PadLeft(s string, width int) string for left-aligned and right-aligned field padding within a fixed-width column. Padding is computed from display width, not byte count. Per prd003-format R1.2.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Compute column display width from rune count, not byte count, so multi-byte Unicode characters are measured correctly. Per prd003-format R1.3.\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Implement FileTypeColor(mode os.FileMode) string returning the ANSI escape sequence for a file's type, and a Reset constant or function returning the ANSI reset sequence (\\\"\\\\033[0m\\\"). Use GNU ls LS_COLORS default color assignments: directory=34 (blue), symlink=36 (cyan), executable=32 (green), block device=33;1 (yellow bold), char device=33;1 (yellow bold), socket=35 (magenta), pipe=33 (yellow), regular=0 (default). Per prd003-format R2.1, R2.2, R2.4.\"\n      - id: R5\n        text: \"Implement ColorEnabled(w io.Writer) bool that returns true only when w is a TTY. Delegate the TTY check to pkg/sys.IsTerminal (prd002-sys R1.3). When ColorEnabled returns false, FileTypeColor must return an empty string and Reset must return an empty string. Per prd003-format R2.3.\"\n      - id: R6\n        text: \"Implement HumanSize(bytes int64, opts HumanSizeOpts) string that converts a byte count to a human-readable string. HumanSizeOpts supports two modes: binary (1024-based, suffixes K/M/G/T/P/E, default) and SI (1000-based, suffixes kB/MB/GB/TB). Format to at most one decimal place when the value is not an integer at the chosen unit (e.g., 1536 -> \\\"1.5K\\\"). Return \\\"0\\\" for zero byte count regardless of mode. Per prd003-format R3.1, R3.2, R3.3, R3.4.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"ColorEnabled delegates TTY detection to pkg/sys.IsTerminal (prd003-format R2.3). Must not import golang.org/x/term; pkg/sys uses golang.org/x/sys/unix for the TIOCGWINSZ ioctl check (prd002-sys R1.3).\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Color assignments use GNU ls LS_COLORS defaults exactly as specified in prd003-format R2.4. No LS_COLORS environment variable parsing (prd003-format non-goals).\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Columns computes per-column widths from the longest entry in each column, not the global maximum (prd003-format R1.4). This matches GNU ls multi-column layout behavior.\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"File organization mirrors the three capability areas in the PRD: columns.go for column alignment (R1), color.go for ANSI color (R2), humansize.go for size conversion (R3). The package declaration is 'package format' per ARCHITECTURE.yaml project_structure.\"\n      - id: D5\n        text: \"ColorEnabled uses an io.Writer parameter. To extract the file descriptor for IsTerminal, type-assert to *os.File. When the assertion fails (e.g., bufio.Writer wrapping a non-file), return false. This matches the expected usage pattern where stdout is passed directly.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"Columns(entries, 80) with a list of entries produces a [][]string where each row fits within 80 characters and the number of columns is maximized.\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"PadRight(\\\"abc\\\", 10) returns \\\"abc       \\\" (7 trailing spaces). PadLeft(\\\"42\\\", 6) returns \\\"    42\\\" (4 leading spaces).\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"FileTypeColor returns the correct ANSI escape sequence for each of the eight file types: directory (\\\\033[34m), symlink (\\\\033[36m), executable (\\\\033[32m), block device (\\\\033[33;1m), char device (\\\\033[33;1m), socket (\\\\033[35m), pipe (\\\\033[33m), regular (empty string).\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"ColorEnabled returns false when passed an io.Writer backed by an os.Pipe (not a TTY). FileTypeColor returns empty string and Reset returns empty string when color is not enabled.\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"HumanSize(1536, HumanSizeOpts{Binary: true}) returns \\\"1.5K\\\". HumanSize(1000000, HumanSizeOpts{SI: true}) returns \\\"1.0MB\\\". HumanSize(0, HumanSizeOpts{}) returns \\\"0\\\". HumanSize(1024, HumanSizeOpts{Binary: true}) returns \\\"1.0K\\\".\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"All pkg/format code compiles on both darwin/arm64 and linux/amd64 with no build errors, no cgo, and no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n      - id: AC7\n        text: \"go vet ./pkg/format/... passes with no errors.\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":128094,"cache_read_input_tokens":18565,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":128094},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"daebe5a8-f5ff-41e3-974a-afe47dde16e5","uuid":"390aa461-a7ff-464f-8a3d-2e94a4fae0f7"}
